{"name":"Parachute Purple","hex":"#362852","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"parachute","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A device, generally constructed from fabric, that is designed to employ air resistance to control the fall of an object or person, causing them to float instead of falling.","A web or fold of skin extending between the legs of gliding mammals, such as the flying squirrel and colugo."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To jump, fall, descend, etc. using such a device.","To introduce into a place using such a device."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/parachute"},{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Parachute","blocks":[{"text":"A parachute is a device designed to slow an object's descent through an atmosphere by creating drag or aerodynamic lift. It is primarily used to safely support people exiting aircraft at height, but also serves various purposes like slowing cargo, aiding in space capsule recovery on Earth, landing spacecraft on other planets, and stabilizing vehicles or objects. Modern parachutes are typically made from durable fabrics like nylon and come in various shapes, such as dome-shaped, rectangular, and inverted domes, depending on their specific function."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Bangladesh_Air_Force_%28BAF%29_paratroopers_jump_from_a_U.S._Air_Force_C-130_Hercules_aircraft_over_Bangladesh_during_exercise_Cope_South_14_Nov._10%2C_2013_131110-F-SI013-240.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parachute","wikidata":"Q482816"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg/500px-Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg/500px-Conical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The oldest known depiction of a parachute, attributed to Francesco di Giorgio Martini (Italy, 1470s)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AConical_Parachute%2C_1470s%2C_British_Museum_Add._MSS_34%2C113%2C_fol._200v.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Homo_Volans.jpg/500px-Homo_Volans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Homo_Volans.jpg/1280px-Homo_Volans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Homo_Volans.jpg/500px-Homo_Volans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Faust Vrančić's parachute design, titled Homo Volans (\"Flying Man\"), from his Machinae Novae (\"New Contraptions\", published in 1615 or 1616)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHomo_Volans.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Early_flight_02561u_%283%29.jpg/500px-Early_flight_02561u_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Early_flight_02561u_%283%29.jpg/1280px-Early_flight_02561u_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Early_flight_02561u_%283%29.jpg/500px-Early_flight_02561u_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Louis-Sébastien Lenormand jumps from the tower of the Montpellier observatory, 1783. Illustration from the late 19th century.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEarly_flight_02561u_(3).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Early_flight_02561u_%284%29.jpg/500px-Early_flight_02561u_%284%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Early_flight_02561u_%284%29.jpg/500px-Early_flight_02561u_%284%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" The first use of a frameless parachute, by André Garnerin in 1797","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File